# AUTHORED COLOR TRANSITIONS
# apply(scene, step) is a lookup expressed as branches with literal colors.
# scene chooses one of seven movements. step 0 is its own palette; steps 1..16
# walk toward the next movement's colors. The final movement returns to the
# opening scheme. main selects those steps during its last 256 display frames.
#
# bg_palette(slot, color0, color1, color2, color3) overwrites four colors in a
# Color background palette slot. Here each scene uses its corresponding slot
# 1..7, matching the clip's palette assignment. Slot 0 is reserved separately
# in visuals.initialize(). Hex colors are converted to the console's available
# color precision, so not every desktop RGB shade remains distinct.
#
# These branches execute at runtime, but the interpolation was authored ahead
# of time. No RGB interpolation loop runs per pixel or frame. A palette change
# recolors existing pattern indices; it cannot move an edge or morph a shape.
# The prepared transition pictures supply shape changes at the same time.
# On monochrome Game Boy the demo uses grayscale; these Color palette steps
# do not turn monochrome hardware into a color display. Replacing this data is
# optional artistic work, not something a basic Pyson game must implement.

# Literal palette steps keep hardware color details out of the timeline.
def apply(scene, step):
    if scene == 0:
        if step == 0:
            bg_palette(1, "#dffaff", "#7793c8", "#27334f", "#03050e")
        elif step == 1:
            bg_palette(1, "#e1fafb", "#7d94c1", "#2b3450", "#03050e")
        elif step == 2:
            bg_palette(1, "#e3f9f7", "#8395ba", "#2f3552", "#03050f")
        elif step == 3:
            bg_palette(1, "#e5f9f3", "#8996b4", "#323653", "#03050f")
        elif step == 4:
            bg_palette(1, "#e7f9ef", "#8f97ad", "#363754", "#04060f")
        elif step == 5:
